# Pi sandbox and permissions

Env-specific facts; decisions and their triggers live in the chezmoi repo's `docs/decisions.md`.
If behavior seems changed, verify against the source and update this file.

## Planning and permissions (last verified 2026-09-18)

- New sessions start in plan mode with auto-reviewed approvals. In the normal
  sandbox, source writes stay disabled; build/cache artifacts use designated
  writable roots. A reviewed unsandboxed plan action is not OS-enforced read-only.
- Plan mode admits only configured read-only child roles; writers (including
  `general-purpose`) are refused before launch. Approval restores writer
  admission without widening read-only roles.
- `/execute` or approval of `submit_plan` enables scoped execution. Every mode
  switch (`/plan`, `/execute`, plan approval) first revokes child sessions and
  sandbox processes, so a child still running when the plan is approved ends
  aborted. An approval is single-use: a mode change before its call runs voids it.
- Root `workspace_write`/`workspace_edit` stay listed in plan mode, as in Claude
  Code, and are refused (`Writes are disabled in this scope`) until execution is
  approved; `ask_user_question` works in either mode.
- `/approvals` selects auto review or individual prompts. Hard denial, inherited
  role scope and mode restrictions run before approval; approval cannot widen them.
  Dialogs are once-only; MCP approvals are never cached across a mode change.
- Shell commands run inside the SRT profile without review, as sandboxed commands
  do in Claude Code; the profile is the boundary. Only remote-mutating verbs it
  cannot judge — `git push`, `gh` other than view/list/status/search/diff/checks,
  `gh api` with a method or field, `npm publish`, `docker push`, `curl`/`wget`
  with data, json or upload flags, `ssh`, `scp`, `sftp`, `rsync` — and `git commit`
  (the user commits) go to the classifier, then a confirm dialog on `ask`.
  Workspace file edits run without review once execution is approved.
- Classifier errors/uncertainty prompt the root session; unattended requests deny.
  A pending dialog is announced in the footer and times out after ten minutes.
- Inside the profile the environment is the host's minus the workflow's own
  variables, secret-named exports (`KEY|SECRET|TOKEN|PASSW|CREDENTIAL|AUTH`) and
  pre-sandbox hooks (`BASH_ENV`, `ENV`, `SHELLOPTS`, `PS4`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`);
  toolchain variables such as `GOPATH` arrive as exported.
- `workspace_bash` with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` (Claude Code's flag) runs
  on the host, environment minus the workflow's own variables. Every such call is
  reviewed, plan mode included (classifier under auto, else the confirm dialog);
  read-only roles are refused; the row reads `Ran … · unsandboxed`. Set it only
  after a sandbox-attributable failure of that command or on request, per command.
  The docker socket is one: no `--config`/`DOCKER_HOST` workaround reaches it.
- Temp files: `$TMPDIR` is the lease's scratch directory, so `mktemp -d
  "$TMPDIR/x.XXXXXX"` works while a bare `mktemp -d` (macOS ignores `TMPDIR`
  without a template) hits the denied `/var/folders`. Apple's `/bin/bash` 3.2
  puts here-documents in `/var/tmp`, writable for every role.
- `run_in_background: true` on `workspace_bash` holds the call 10 s: a command
  ending inside that is answered like a foreground call and never notifies.
  A longer one returns its task id; at exit the model hears the
  status and last twenty lines (with its next tool result, or as a new turn).
  `workspace_task` `{ id, action: "output" | "stop" }` reads or stops it, `{ action:
  "list" }` lists tasks; output is tail-capped at 256 KiB. `/plan`, `/remove-dir`
  and exiting pi stop tasks; detached `setsid` daemons and containers outlive that.
  If a mode change is blocked on unverified cleanup, kill orphans and restart.
- `/add-dir <path>` adds an editable directory, as Claude Code's does: edits need
  approved execution, its `.git`, `.pi` and relative denies (`.env`) are protected,
  its `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` joins the system prompt. Home, its ancestors, denied
  paths, symlinks out and directories already in scope are refused with the reason.
  `/remove-dir` drops one; state is per session; its skills need a restart with
  `--skill <path>`. Reads were never workspace-confined; only the deny list applies.
- Playwright alone runs outside SRT so Chromium can launch, with only PATH, HOME
  and the broker's scratch TMPDIR; its disposable profile is not containment.
  Approvals, denials, role checks and cleanup still apply.

Interactive `!` commands are the user's own (Claude Code's `!`): pi's local shell
on the host, the host environment minus the workflow's own variables, no sandbox,
no review, in any mode.

Sensitive paths come from the shared denylist. File tools resolve symlinks and
confine edits to the workspace; git metadata and live-loaded code (`~/.pi/agent`,
the repository `node_modules`, `~/.zshrc`) are write-protected in every mode, the
repository source editable once execution is approved. A refusal names its reason
(`Writes are disabled in this scope`, `Path denied by managed policy`, `File edits
must remain inside the workspace`); report it rather than re-submit the plan. The
denylist does not cover unknown credential locations: add new ones to shared
policy before exposing them to a session.

Extensions are trusted host code; per-tool SRT is not containment against a
malicious one. macOS needs sandbox-exec.
